1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,978
Who is it?

2
00:00:02,978 --> 00:00:03,728
Dad.

3
00:00:03,728 --> 00:00:06,206
Come on in, Dad.

4
00:00:06,206 --> 00:00:10,601
I thought you might be hungry.

5
00:00:10,601 --> 00:00:15,557
I brought you a chicken sandwich and a glass of milk.

6
00:00:15,557 --> 00:00:17,868
I am hungry.

7
00:00:17,868 --> 00:00:18,668
Thanks, Dad.

8
00:00:18,668 --> 00:00:23,824
What time is it, anyway?

9
00:00:23,824 --> 00:00:25,024
Ten o'clock.

10
00:00:25,024 --> 00:00:27,402
What are you working on?

11
00:00:27,402 --> 00:00:30,740
I'm writing a story for the high-school paper.

12
00:00:30,740 --> 00:00:33,334
Can't you finish it tomorrow?

13
00:00:33,334 --> 00:00:36,486
No, I have to turn it in in the morning.

14
00:00:36,486 --> 00:00:39,158
What's it about?

15
00:00:39,158 --> 00:00:43,092
I'm writing an article on the feelings about graduation.

16
00:00:43,092 --> 00:00:46,669
And ... how do you feel?

17
00:00:46,669 --> 00:00:47,647
Me?

18
00:00:47,647 --> 00:00:50,625
A little scared.

19
00:00:50,625 --> 00:00:53,603
And excited, too.

20
00:00:53,603 --> 00:00:56,403
I felt the same way.

21
00:00:56,403 --> 00:00:58,295
The scary part's leaving home and moving to college.

22
00:00:58,295 --> 00:01:05,364
Oh, leaving home is part of growing up.

23
00:01:05,364 --> 00:01:07,515
Well, don't work all night.

24
00:01:07,515 --> 00:01:08,493
I don't mind.

25
00:01:08,493 --> 00:01:10,493
I enjoy writing.

26
00:01:10,493 --> 00:01:10,893
Well,

27
00:01:10,893 --> 00:01:12,934
maybe you should think about becoming a writer.

28
00:01:12,934 --> 00:01:14,449
Maybe I should.

29
00:01:14,449 --> 00:01:17,427
You have lots of time to decide.

30
00:01:17,427 --> 00:01:20,405
That's the worst part--making decisions.

31
00:01:20,405 --> 00:01:23,383
You'll be OK.

32
00:01:23,383 --> 00:01:24,543
Good night, Son.

33
00:01:24,543 --> 00:01:36,035
Good night, Dad.

34
00:01:36,035 --> 00:01:37,885
"Hail to the victors valiant,

35
00:01:37,885 --> 00:01:40,273
Hail to the conquering heroes,

36
00:01:40,273 --> 00:01:42,531
Hail, Hail to Michigan,

37
00:01:42,531 --> 00:01:44,229
The champions of the West!"

38
00:01:44,229 --> 00:01:48,207
Ah, good morning, Robbie.

39
00:01:48,207 --> 00:01:49,207
Good morning, Dad.

40
00:01:49,207 --> 00:01:50,185
How's my grandson?

41
00:01:50,185 --> 00:01:53,161
Fine, Grandpa. Fine!

42
00:01:53,161 --> 00:01:54,051
What's all the cheering about?

43
00:01:54,051 --> 00:01:57,073
Did the University of Michigan win another football game?

44
00:01:57,073 --> 00:01:58,207
Better than that.

45
00:01:58,207 --> 00:02:00,011
Tell him, Philip.

46
00:02:00,011 --> 00:02:01,841
I just spoke with Charley Rafer.

47
00:02:01,841 --> 00:02:03,429
Who's Charley Rafer?

48
00:02:03,429 --> 00:02:07,674
He's the Dean of Admissions for the University of Michigan.

49
00:02:07,674 --> 00:02:09,688
And it turns out he's a classmate of Philip's.

50
00:02:09,688 --> 00:02:11,750
We were both on the tennis team.

51
00:02:11,750 --> 00:02:12,110
Great!

52
00:02:12,110 --> 00:02:14,008
It is great.

53
00:02:14,008 --> 00:02:17,912
He's going to be in New York tomorrow

54
00:02:17,912 --> 00:02:18,112
to interview applicants for admission.

55
00:02:18,106 --> 00:02:21,740
And he's agreed to fit you into his schedule.

56
00:02:21,740 --> 00:02:24,609
But I may not want to go to Michigan.

57
00:02:24,609 --> 00:02:27,936
It's one of the best schools in the country, Robbie.

58
00:02:27,936 --> 00:02:29,198
I studied medicine there.

59
00:02:29,198 --> 00:02:31,598
Your grandfather went to the Engineering School there.

60
00:02:31,598 --> 00:02:33,276
I know that, but ...

61
00:02:33,276 --> 00:02:36,919
You said you wanted to be a doctor like your father.

62
00:02:36,919 --> 00:02:37,436
Not exactly.

63
00:02:37,436 --> 00:02:41,386
You couldn't pick a finer medical school than Michigan.

64
00:02:41,386 --> 00:02:42,538
Yes, I know that.

65
00:02:42,538 --> 00:02:45,502
Let's meet with Charley at the university club.

66
00:02:45,502 --> 00:02:47,266
Ten o'clock tomorrow morning.

67
00:02:47,266 --> 00:02:51,458
It doesn't mean you're going to Michigan.

68
00:02:51,458 --> 00:02:54,055
It doesn't mean you have to be a doctor.

69
00:02:54,055 --> 00:02:57,500
But the interview will be good experience for you.

70
00:02:57,500 --> 00:02:59,078
In that case, it's OK.

71
00:02:59,078 --> 00:03:01,656
Dad, growing up means

72
00:03:01,656 --> 00:03:05,611
making my own decisions, doesn't it?

73
00:03:05,611 --> 00:03:06,588
You're right, Robbie.

74
00:03:06,588 --> 00:03:10,589
But, like your Grandpa suggested,

75
00:03:10,589 --> 00:03:12,344
have the interview.

76
00:03:12,344 --> 00:03:17,361
And then make your own decision.

77
00:03:17,361 --> 00:03:19,523
That sounds fine.

78
00:03:19,523 --> 00:03:23,241
I know it's sudden, Robbie,

79
00:03:23,241 --> 00:03:25,479
but this is an important opportunity.

80
00:03:25,479 --> 00:03:28,457
We'll head down there first thing tomorrow morning.

81
00:03:28,457 --> 00:03:28,964
OK?

82
00:03:28,964 --> 00:03:31,435
Sure, Dad.

83
00:03:31,435 --> 00:03:37,391
I want you to know something, Son.

84
00:03:37,391 --> 00:03:41,377
I'm ... very proud of you.

85
00:03:41,377 --> 00:03:42,049
Thanks, Dad.

86
00:03:42,049 --> 00:03:47,661
Well, I've got an appointment at the hospital.

87
00:03:47,661 --> 00:03:49,303
I'll see you all at dinnertime.

88
00:03:49,303 --> 00:03:51,083
Bye, Dad.

89
00:03:51,083 --> 00:03:56,334
Is something still wrong, Robbie?

90
00:03:56,334 --> 00:03:58,237
I'll be OK.

91
00:03:58,237 --> 00:04:04,115
Going away to college for the first time

92
00:04:04,115 --> 00:04:07,171
always makes one a little nervous.

93
00:04:07,171 --> 00:04:11,009
I guess so.

94
00:04:11,009 --> 00:04:14,009
I'll be OK. I just need time to think.
